```

`ord` is the same idea as the path array, built by PostgreSQL. With BREADTH, `ord` becomes `(depth, id)` pairs and the first five rows are the roots 1, 2, 7, 9 and 11. The walk up returns 13 (Mateo, 0 hops), 8, 4 and then Ben Carter with `referred_by` ∅ and 3 hops. The next round looks for `customer_id = NULL`, which matches nothing, so it is empty and the loop stops.

code.src:
BEGIN;
UPDATE customers SET referred_by = 6 WHERE customer_id = 1;   -- Asha "referred by" Farid
SET LOCAL statement_timeout = '3s';
WITH RECURSIVE downline AS (
    SELECT customer_id, 0 AS depth FROM customers WHERE customer_id = 1
  UNION ALL
    SELECT c.customer_id, d.depth + 1
    FROM customers c JOIN downline d ON c.referred_by = d.customer_id
)
SELECT count(*) FROM downline;
ROLLBACK;

BEGIN;
UPDATE customers SET referred_by = 6 WHERE customer_id = 1;
WITH RECURSIVE downline AS (
    SELECT customer_id, 0 AS depth FROM customers WHERE customer_id = 1
  UNION ALL
    SELECT c.customer_id, d.depth + 1
    FROM customers c JOIN downline d ON c.referred_by = d.customer_id
) CYCLE customer_id SET is_cycle USING path
SELECT customer_id, depth, is_cycle, path FROM downline;

WITH RECURSIVE downline AS (
    SELECT customer_id FROM customers WHERE customer_id = 1
  UNION
    SELECT c.customer_id FROM customers c JOIN downline d ON c.referred_by = d.customer_id
)
SELECT customer_id FROM downline;
ROLLBACK;

try.expect:
After three seconds the first query fails with `ERROR:  canceling statement due to statement timeout`. The CYCLE version stops on its own:

```postgresql
 customer_id | depth | is_cycle |       path
-------------+-------+----------+-------------------
           1 |     0 | f        | {(1)}
           3 |     1 | f        | {(1),(3)}
           5 |     1 | f        | {(1),(5)}
           6 |     2 | f        | {(1),(3),(6)}
          10 |     2 | f        | {(1),(5),(10)}
           1 |     3 | t        | {(1),(3),(6),(1)}
(6 rows)
```

Asha comes back at depth 3 with `is_cycle` true and is not followed again. The UNION version returns 1, 3, 5, 6 and 10: the round that found Asha again added nothing new, so the next working table was empty. Both ROLLBACKs restore Asha's real `referred_by`, NULL.
